TOKYO (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) on Tuesday forecast that Asia's emerging economies will grow 5.2 percent this year, slower than in 2023 but higher than the three-month forecast. The economy is stronger than expected in China. According to Reuters.
Asia's economic growth forecast for 2024, up from 5.4 percent last year, was 0.4 percent higher than in October last year.
By 2025, developing Asia is expected to grow by 4.5 percent, according to an updated report by the International Monetary Fund (IMF) on the global economic outlook.
China's growth forecast for this year has been raised by 0.4 percent to 4.6 percent due to rising government spending, even though it was lower than last year's 5.2 percent. .
China's economy could recover faster than expected if Beijing makes further reforms in the real estate sector, such as restructuring real estate developers that go bankrupt or spend more than expected, the IMF said. Increase user confidence.
But the IMF also warned of the risk that China's growth could be lower than expected if authorities resort to "unintentional fiscal tightening" that could hurt consumption. According to Reuters.
"In the absence of a comprehensive restructuring policy package for troubled real estate, real estate investment could fall short of expectations and last longer with negative implications for domestic growth," the IMF added. And business partners. ”
