BANGKOK: Thailand's economy is in a recession due to high household debt, which has put pressure on the central bank to cut interest rates, the Thai Deputy Finance Minister said on Monday. According to Reuters.
He said Thailand's policy rate, which is the highest in a decade at 2.5 percent, should be reduced at the next central bank review policy meeting scheduled for May 7. February to help reduce borrowing costs.
"The rate should be lowered because high interest rates are now a burden on the people," Julapun was quoted as saying by Reuters. "People can not survive."
Recently, Thai Prime Minister Setha Thavisin also urged the Bank of Thailand to cut its benchmark interest rate to help boost Southeast Asia's second-largest economy, which he says is in crisis.
The central bank kept its policy rate unchanged at 2.5 percent at its last meeting in November after raising 200 basis points since August 2022 to curb inflation.
