In a crucial decision, 15 of the 17 judges voted in favor of emergency measures that cover most of what South Africa has requested, except for an order not to mention Israeli military action in Gaza.
In Friday's ruling, the judge said Israel must take all measures within its power to prevent and punish its troops from committing genocide and must take steps to improve the humanitarian situation there.
"The State of Israel shall take all possible measures within the bounds of its powers to prevent all acts falling under Article 2 of the 1948 Genocide Convention," the court said.
However, the International Court of Justice (ICJ) has not ordered a ceasefire in Gaza, but has said it will not rule out genocide, ruling that Palestine is a protected group under Gaza. Genocide Convention of 1948. The court also said it recognized the rights of the Palestinian people in Gaza to be protected from genocide.
In late December, South Africa filed a lawsuit with the International Court of Justice (ICJ) alleging that Israel had committed genocide against Palestinians in its war against Hamas. Gaza has also asked the International Court of Justice to order Israel to immediately suspend its military activities.
