Fisheries Administration calls on those who are raising fish or aquatic animals of foreign origin to take care to avoid entering the natural waters.
The Fisheries Administration is pleased and thankful to the people, especially the fishermen who participated in releasing rare species of fish, species and other aquatic animals such as royal fish, carp, fish, cucumbers, turtles, royal turtles, sea dolphins, which is a great activity. It is good to participate in the protection and protection of fishery resources and to receive great congratulations from the masses.
Recently, some people have released fish of foreign origin into natural waters, this activity may pose a risk to local fish.
The Fisheries Administration has stated that some species of aquatic animals or fish of foreign origin are ferocious, carnivorous or fast-breeding. Nature takes over the habitat and can affect local breeds.
In this regard, the Fisheries Administration calls on those who are raising fish or aquatic animals of foreign origin to take care to avoid releasing or neglecting to enter the natural waters. If you wish to release fish or aquatic animals from abroad, please contact the Fisheries Officer at the following telephone number.
The Fisheries Administration would like to encourage the people, especially fishermen, to continue to provide information to the fisheries authorities and to release rare species of fish, fish species and other aquatic animals that have been revived, which is an invaluable activity. Cost to participate in the care, protection and conservation of our fishery resources to survive for future generations.
