According to the National Bank of Cambodia's annual report for 2023 and 2024, "Cambodia's international reserves have reached approximately US $ 20 billion, an increase of 12.3% year on year. 2022 will be equal to more than 7 months of imports of goods and services next time.
She added that exchange rate stability has contributed significantly to strengthening investor confidence, partially controlling inflationary pressures, as well as maintaining macroeconomic stability. The exchange rate is considered by economists as an indicator of economic health, as former IMF Managing Director Per Jacobson once said that raising the value of the national currency well is a priority in building national economic development. .
"The uncertainty of international financial markets and the maintenance of high benchmark interest rates in developed countries have led to a significant reduction in the international reserves of some central banks," she said.
According to the International Monetary Fund (IMF), the international reserves of a country refer to the internationally recognized assets that a country has and is controlled by the monetary authority for use in case of need, such as: financing of balances if required, Exchange market intervention to manage exchange rate fluctuations and maintain confidence in the national currency and economy.
For developing countries like Cambodia, the minimum adequacy level of international reserves should be 3 months of imports of goods and services in the future. The reason why the IMF prefers to compare international reserves to imports is because imports are a demand for foreign goods and services that a country needs to supply the economy adequately in addition to domestic production.
