Kiev initially acknowledged that there would be a hostage exchange with Russia on Wednesday, but hours after the crash, Ukraine said it had no reliable information on the passengers on board. Ukraine's Volodymyr Zelensky has called for an international investigation.
Russia's Defense Ministry says the crashed IL-76 military plane was carrying 65 Ukrainian prisoners of war, along with a total of six crew and pilots, as well as three guards escorting the prisoners. According to the Russian Defense Ministry, Ukrainian forces stationed in the Kharkiv region near the border fired two missiles at the transport aircraft and described the incident as an act of terrorism.
Ukraine's intelligence agency says it does not yet have reliable or comprehensive information on the people on board the Russian plane, such as who they are and how many. Zelensky did not acknowledge or deny Moscow's allegations, but stressed that Russia was playing with the life of Ukrainian prisoners of war, as well as the feelings of their relatives and the Ukrainian society as a whole.
