Pursat Provincial Administration Holds a Meeting to Discuss Water Allocation from January 27 to February 8 to Intervene to Save Dry Rice
(Pursat): Mr. Lay Viseth, Deputy Governor, represented by Mr. Khoy Rida, Governor of Pursat Province, on January 24, 2024, chaired a meeting on water use discussion from January 27 to February 08 to Intervention to save dry season rice farmers who need water as they exceed the plan.
The meeting was held at the meeting hall of the Provincial Department of Water Resources and Meteorology with the participation of relevant departments and authorities of Bakan District, Phnom Kravanh District, Kandeang District, Talo Sen Chey District and Pursat City.
According to Mr. Keo Vay, Director of the Provincial Department of Water Resources and Meteorology, we have the third and fifth dams to intervene to save dry rice, but we have already opened the fifth dam twice. Now we are opening the third dam, using 40%, only 60% left.
He said that the leadership of the Ministry has made a plan to Samdech Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech decided to build a second dam immediately as a priority of Pursat province, because Pursat province, we face a shortage of water for dry rice fields in the provinces. Various.
He asked all commune chiefs and village chiefs to consider the technical standards from the provincial leaders and specialized departments to disseminate to farmers to avoid exceeding the plan like this year, which leads to budget losses.
At the same time, commune chiefs, commune chiefs and water users must be smart in communicating with the business team and contributing to the people to restore natural lakes, restore shallow canals or garbage clogged waterways.
It should be informed that in 2023-2024, due to the increase in rice prices, farmers have rushed to dry up rice fields, which is why there is a shortage of water in the middle of the year.
According to the plan of the Provincial Department of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries throughout the province, the farmers next to the water source can only produce 20,000 hectares of dry rice, but as of January 08, 2024, our farmers have done more than 50,000 hectares, which is 271% more than planned.
