A meeting to review the results of monthly and annual tax collection management in 2023 and the direction for further implementation of the General Department of Taxation was chaired by Dr. Aun Porn Moniroth, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Economy and Finance on the 23rd. January 2024.
According to the General Department of Taxation, in the first 12 months of 2023, the actual tax revenue collected through the online revenue data management system was 14,629.10 billion riels (approximately 3,612.12 million dollars), equivalent to 101.13 % Of the Financial Law Plan for Management 2023 compared to tax revenue in the same period of 2022 is an increase of 628.28 billion Riels (approximately 155.13 million USD) equal to 4.49 %.
Mr. Kong Vibol, Minister Delegate to the Prime Minister and Delegate of the Royal Government in charge of the Director General of the General Department of Taxation, emphasized that this successful and proud result is an important contribution to the continued development of the nation.
It should also be noted that 2023 is the fifth year and the last year of the five-year revenue collection strategy 2019-2023 and 2024 is the first year of the implementation of the five-year revenue collection strategy 2024-2028.
Overall, the General Department of Taxation has successfully implemented the Royal Government's Revenue Collection Strategy 2019-2023, which focuses on updating and modernizing tax policy and administration in order to increase the capacity, efficiency and effectiveness of revenue collection management. Taxes.
The success of tax revenue management for five consecutive years is a real fruit of strengthening the capacity and management system of the tax administration, increasing awareness of tax culture and increasing the level of compliance by taxpayers. Senior Minister Osman Hassan pays a courtesy call on Secretary-General of the Southern Cooperation Organization
The meeting was held at the office of Senior Minister Osman Hassan in Phnom Penh in order to strengthen and expand cooperation in all fields.
In response, Dr. Osman Hassan expressed his happiness and support for the initiative of the Southern Cooperation Organization (OSC) and called for stronger cooperation with the Cambodian government in all areas.
(Phnom Penh): The families of the victims of a traffic accident in Skor Dach Village, Sangkat Takeo, Prey Veng City, Prey Veng Province expressed their happiness and gratitude to the Ministry of Justice and the judiciary for expediting the settlement of their case. He and give justice to both parties.
Through a video obtained by Fresh News on January 24, 2024, the victim thanked all relevant authorities for strictly following the strict regulations of the management to expedite the settlement of the victims. Traffic offenses are a priority in the process of implementing the campaign set by the Ministry of Justice.
It should be informed that on December 3, 2023 at 8:15 pm, a traffic accident occurred in Skor Dach Village, Sangkat Ta Kor, Prey Veng City, Prey Veng Province, in which a 17-year-old boy was riding a motorcycle to touch the victim. The victim was a 45-year-old man who was traveling on the same side of the road. The victim was taken to a hospital and received partial assistance from the PRC. After the incident, the victim filed a complaint with the Prey Veng Provincial Prosecutor's Office.
Immediately after the Prey Veng Provincial Prosecutor's Office took charge of Traffic Case No. 1126 dated December 18, 2023, the working group tried to mediate between the perpetrator and the victim for a whole day until Agree between both parties.
Through negotiations and mediation, the perpetrators agreed to pay 18 million riels in compensation to the victims, providing 12 million riels in advance and promising to repay the remaining 6 million riels within 20 months. Separately, the victim party agreed to receive this compensation and withdrew the complaint.
The relatives of the victim are very happy and express their gratitude and express their trust in the judiciary that has given justice to him and his family without spending a single riel on the procedure of Resolving his case in court.
They also thanked the Prosecutor of the Prey Veng Provincial Prosecutor's Office and all the competent authorities for implementing the strict regulations of the leadership to expedite the settlement of victims in traffic accidents. Traffic offenses are a priority in the process of implementing the campaign set by the Ministry of Justice.
It should be informed that the Ministry of Justice has set up a working group to receive complaints from citizens in the campaign to promote and expedite the settlement of court cases and the campaign to check and resolve irregularities in the court, with the telephone number 099 444 181.
Crack down on large-scale cross-border drug trafficking case, arrest 5 foreigners and confiscate more than 1.5 tons of drugs
(Phnom Penh): The Anti-Drug Department conducted an operation to crack down on cross-border drug trafficking, arrested five foreigners and confiscated more than 1.5 tons of drugs.
The crackdown was carried out on the orders of General Sar Thet, National Police Commissioner General, General Mak Chito, and Lt. Gen. Prum Santhor, Deputy Commissioner General of the Anti-Drug Crime Plan, under the direct command of Lt. Gen. Kheung Sarath, Director of the Anti-Drug Department. Addicted.
Suspects and exhibits seized above, the competent authorities are building a case to send to the Sihanoukville Court according to the procedure.
Please be informed that before reaching the crackdown on drug trafficking cases, the Anti-Drug Crime Force has been investigating and monitoring the targets for more than a year.
It should be noted that General Sar Thet, the National Police Commissioner General, has issued strict orders and instructed the Anti-Drug Department to clear all drug criminals.
Receiving this order, the Anti-Drug Department, led by Lt. Gen. Khueng Sarath, is determined to crack down on drugs in the capitals and provinces as fast as it can and crack down to the roots.
Recently, many major drug trafficking cases have been cracked down on, and many criminals have been arrested and taken to court.
A senior official of the Ministry of Interior told the officials at the international border checkpoint to try to resolve the complaints of the workers
(Koh Kong): Mr. Samrith Dy, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Deputy Chairman of the Commission for the Inspection and Solving of the Migration of Cambodian Workers, told the officers at the international border checkpoint to pay attention. To resolve complaints of Cambodian workers crossing the border, there must be an immediate response system.
This statement was made during a meeting to inspect the entry and exit of workers in Koh Kong province and was attended by Ms. Mithona Phuthong, Governor of Koh Kong Province, as well as subordinates. The meeting was held at the Cham Yeam Immigration Police Station on January 23, 2024.
At the same time, Mr. Samrith Dy also asked the Koh Kong Provincial Authority and the specialized units at the Cham Yeam International Border Crossing to continue to actively participate in capturing information and facilitating the entry and exit of workers and resolving all complaints. Better.
Mr. Samrith Dy explained that all receiving and resolving complaints of Cambodian workers related to border crossings must have an emergency response system to facilitate workers to communicate and report irregularities at international border crossings.
Mr. Samrith Dy also instructed the Commission to monitor and resolve the entry and exit of Cambodian workers in Koh Kong Province to put in place operational measures or issue instructions to eliminate unofficial fees for entry and exit travel. Border gates are intended to facilitate the entry and exit of workers at the border.
The governor of Koh Kong province claims that the provincial authorities have taken measures to prevent illegal border crossings and crack down on all kinds of crimes to maintain security and public order to the people more effectively.
(Phnom Penh): Justin Wyatt, Australian Ambassador to Cambodia, on January 23, 2024, visited the Marie Stopes International Cambodia (MSIC) clinic in Sen Sok district in accordance with the government's commitment. Australia in promoting health and the rights of women and groups in need of support in Cambodia.
Marie Stopes International Cambodia is a leading organization in Cambodia in contraception, family planning and safe abortion services, providing comprehensive healthcare services for women of all ages.
Marie Stopes International Cambodia supports all women and provides high quality, affordable, confidential and non-judgmental health care services, ensuring that every step of every client's access to health care is done with dignity and respect. Respect.
The Australian Government is funding two projects implemented by Marie Stopes International Cambodia through the first project, "Responses through the provision of key sexual and reproductive health services and new rights and mechanisms for providing health services." Called RESPOND and another project through the Australian NGO Cooperation Program (ANCP).
Both projects aim to increase access to high-quality sexual and reproductive health in all aspects, including family planning for all women of equal reproductive age.
Counseling for women of all ages, from menstruating women to menopause, Marie Stopes International Cambodia focuses on teens, young women, women with disabilities, and other groups in need of support through these projects.
In addition, ongoing support through the ANCP project since 2020 has supported Marie Stopes International Cambodia in opening two new clinics in areas where women are facing challenges. Distance to sexual and reproductive health services.
The two new clinics are located in Khan Sen Sok, Phnom Penh and the other in Poipet. Both projects have contributed to improving the provision of sexual health services and services for victims of gender-based violence (SGBV), as well as strengthening remote medical care and a fee-free system that eliminates financial barriers for groups. People who need support.
The Australian Ambassador's visit to Marie Stopes International Cambodia's Sen Sok Clinic is a testament to the collaboration between Australia and Marie Stopes International Cambodia, reaffirming their joint commitment to supporting access to health care. Sex and reproduction from all angles.
The success of both projects was discussed between the Australian Ambassador and Marie Stopes International Goodwill Ambassador of Cambodia.
"We support all women in making decisions about their lives, including the right to decide to get pregnant," said the Australian Ambassador to Cambodia. This is the reason why access to sexual and reproductive health services is so important. "And that is why we are pleased to partner with Marie Stopes to support safe and high quality health services for women."
