Prime Minister Hun Manet: Law on the Press Made to Protect the Dignity of Journalists (Video inside) (Phnom Penh) No, instead, to protect the rights of journalists, to protect journalists who act in accordance with the law, to protect their values.
This statement was made by the President at the closing ceremony of the 2023 work review meeting and the 2024 work direction of the Ministry of Information on January 23, 2024.
On that occasion, Samdech President encouraged the Ministry of Information to cooperate with other ministries and institutions to prepare a code of conduct, ethics, national journalism, a press charter for the national media and journalists to respect and implement.
"The creation of a law relating to the press is not to block the rights of journalists, but to protect journalists who protect their rights," he said.
He added that through the law on the press, the people will have a better understanding of the rights of journalists and the role of journalists.
Samdech President Hun Manet stated that the Royal Government does not consider journalists as enemies, but is a necessary partner of the Royal Government. In a democracy like Cambodia, the law must be properly enforced so as not to infringe on the rights of others.
Minister of Information Neth Pheaktra said that currently Cambodia has more than 2,000 media outlets and about 10,000 journalists, but only about 4,000 applicants.
