(Uganda): Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, High Representative of the Prime Minister of Cambodia Hun Manet, led a delegation to the 19th NAM Summit. ) Under the theme "Strengthening Cooperation for Global Prosperity", which took place from 19 to 20 January 2024 in Kampala, Republic of Uganda.
On behalf of His Excellency Hun Manet, Dr. Hang Chuon Naron stated that over the past two decades, Cambodia has finally resolved its internal conflicts, implemented a market economy reform program and achieved 7% annual economic growth. Over the last two decades, the country has moved from being a poor country to a low-middle-income country.
Following the July 2023 election, the new Royal Government of Cambodia, under the leadership of His Majesty King Hun Manet, has launched a Pentagon Strategy for Sustainable Development aimed at achieving high-middle-income status by 2030. And the Vision of Cambodia 2050. Realizing the importance of peace in national development, Cambodia declared December 29 as "Peace Day in Cambodia".
Cambodia fully supports the ratification of the 19th Summit of the Heads of State and Government of the Non-Aligned Movement. It will be an important roadmap for strengthening cooperation and co-operation among the Non-Aligned Movement and other countries on political issues, security and socio-economic development and human rights issues on a global scale. Regions and sub-regions.
Cambodia fully supports the theme of the current Summit, which focuses on issues beyond political neutrality and security to promote the building of a global community, shared values, aspirations and a sincere commitment to build a world of We become a better place for all of us and to harmonize with each other in diverse cultures and civilizations.
The statement also focused on reforming international financial institutions and finding solutions to foreign debt management, promoting the successful implementation of Agenda 2023 on Sustainable Development Goals, developing information and communication technology and energy. Trade and technology transfer.
