General knowledge: Before the great era like today, how did the ancient people count the years?
Of course, due to the lack of a global communication system, the ancients did not yet agree on an era for universal chronology, that is, they used different timepieces in different regions. The number of hours, days, months, years and seasons is not the same.
In Europe, the number of days is still uncertain, as the number of days in a year is based on ancient Egyptian customs.
The Egyptian Empire created a solar calendar to divide a year into 13 months. The Egyptian calendar is one of the first calendars known to the world. The ancient Egyptians later found the year in the lunar calendar, dividing it into seasons, months, days, and hours.
As for the Khmer Empire and the territories under the ancient Khmer Empire, there was no AD and Mahamat era yet. The Khmer people have three major eras for daily use, including the Chul era and the Buddhist era.
During the Angkorian and Chulalongkorn eras, it was used in the Hindu capital and in various government institutions throughout the empire. The Buddhist era is often used only for Buddhists, until later, the official use of the Buddhist era.
During the Longvek period, when Portuguese missionaries and other Europeans came to the Kingdom of Cambodia, the Christian era also flowed into Cambodia together, but was not yet widely used. It was not until the French colonial period that the Khmer kingdom gradually used Christendom until today. US military continues airstrikes on Houthi targets in Yemen
