France welcomes Cambodia's commitment to become a carbon neutral country by 2050
(Phnom Penh): The French government welcomes Cambodia's strong commitment to environmental protection, especially to become a carbon neutral country by 2050 through a strategy focused on a clean, green and sustainable environment.
The welcome was made during a meeting between Cambodian Prime Minister Hun Manet, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and French President Emmanuel Macron at the French Government House on January 19, 2024.
According to a statement on the outcome of the meeting, the French side reiterated its readiness to assist Cambodia in its efforts to link rapid economic development with the energy transition. France also mentioned the role of the French Development Agency, which is reflected in the signing of three agreements totaling 215 million euros and French companies in this context.
Regarding this environmental issue, the Prime Minister confirmed to the French side that Cambodia is committed not to develop hydropower on the Mekong River and also not to develop coal-fired power plants.
The Prime Minister also mentioned that Cambodia will join the Paris Treaty for People and the Planet. The two sides also agreed to work together to host the UN Conference on the Ocean to be held in Nice, France in 2025.
It should be noted that in order to become a carbon neutral country, Samdech Hun Sen has set out many environmental policies, including stopping the construction of coal-fired power plants.
