(Phnom Penh): Located in East Asia, Japan has been named one of the countries with the most frequent natural disasters in the world, including 1,500 small and large earthquakes and volcanic eruptions, hurricanes. Floods and other natural disasters strike the island nation every year.
Why is this Asian country experiencing natural disasters so often?
The islands are located in a volcanic belt beneath the Pacific Ocean and have been active since hundreds of millions of years ago. As the magma in the Earth's crust moves, it causes seismic waves beneath the seabed, and in the ocean floor, there is also a seismic movement that causes giant waves to collide with Japanese islands.
Studies have found that some of the factors that cause natural disasters in Japan are due to:
* First: Japan is an archipelago, and the location of the archipelago is far from the continent and is in the direction of the sea breeze, which always blows every year, such as wind, rain, waves, storms, as well. Heavy snowstorms occur in some of the islands of the western Pacific coast.
* Third: Japan is in the earthquake zone, originating under the bottom of the Pacific Ocean, which often causes earthquakes and tsunamis.
* Fourth, Japan is located in the submarine volcanic belt area, where almost all of the world's volcanoes are concentrated along the coast of this ocean, apart from Japan, the main volcanic groups are in the Sumatra archipelago. Of Indonesia, New Zealand, Chile and so on.
In addition, it damaged the Fukushima nuclear plant, causing a crisis in Japan to this day. The 2011 natural disaster became the deadliest natural disaster in Japanese history and ranked fourth in the world since 1900.
According to the 2013 Global Risk Index, Japan has the 15th highest risk of natural disasters in the world. By living a life facing natural disasters all the time, this country has always been strong and ready to protect its people in the most creative way.
