WHO Weighs Artificial Intelligence Risks and Its Benefits for Health Care
GENEVA: The next generation of artificial intelligence could transform healthcare through faster drug development and diagnosis, among others, but the World Health Organization (WHO) on Thursday stressed the need to take action. Pay more attention to its risks. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on January 18, 2024.
The World Health Organization (WHO) is studying the dangers and potential benefits of large multifunctional models of artificial intelligence (LMMs) that are new and rapidly being adopted in the health sector.
"It is predicted that the LMMs model will be widely used and applied in health care, scientific research, public health and drug development," the World Health Organization (WHO) said.
Despite such potential, the WHO warns that there is a risk that the results obtained from the recording of LMMs may produce false, inaccurate or incomplete results. It can also be trained on low quality or biased data related to race, ethnicity, gender, identity, gender or age.
"As artificial intelligence models become more widely used in healthcare, misuse and eventual harm to individuals are inevitable," the WHO warned.
