An Sithav, an economic researcher, explains the Cambodian-French relationship as follows: A Japanese company decides to withdraw from the Malaysia-Singapore high-speed rail project
TOKYO (Reuters) - Japanese companies have decided to drop their bids on a high-speed rail link between Kuala Lumpur and Singapore, government sources said on Thursday. According to the Japanese Kyodo newspaper.
Withdrawal from Japanese companies could allow rival Chinese companies to further strengthen infrastructure in East Asia after they complete the 2023 high-speed rail line in Indonesia. A high-speed train built by a Chinese company through Laos is now underway and work is slowly underway for a project to connect to the line from Thailand.
Next Monday is the deadline for bids for the Malaysia-Singapore high-speed rail line. The Malaysian government began the bidding process in July 2023.
The project is expected to cost 100 billion ringgit ($ 21 billion), but the Malaysian government intends to develop the project through private financing rather than government funding or debt guarantee.
As Japanese companies cancel their bids, some local companies plan to partner with Chinese and European companies to bid, the source told the Kyodo newspaper.
The Malaysian government plans to recruit candidates for the project within a few months and will begin full negotiations with the Singapore government later this year.
