After discussing and reviewing the documents one by one, the NEC meeting decided as follows:
1: Amend and agree in principle on the instructions on the election campaign marches of political parties for the 5th legislative election of the Senate in 2024.
2: Editing and agreeing on the contents of the election security documents for the 5th Senate Election 2024 as follows:
* Decision on permission to use security hand exam for polling security agents at polling stations and counting ballots * Security hand test for identifying polling security agents * Scale leaflets and security system of polling stations and polling stations
3: Agreed on 5 documents for the selection of the composition of the polling station commission and the counting of ballots, a total of 198 people who are short-term contracted officials in the 25 Capital and Provincial Election Commissions for the Senate election. 5 years 2024.
4: In other agendas:
The specialized unit reported on the complaints related to the posting of the initial voter list from 5 to 10 January 2024, with a total of 22 complaints and objections at the Capital and Provincial Election Commission (CEC). 8 BECs that are being resolved for the 5th Senate election. The NEC meeting then discussed the facts and the law involved in resolving the complaints.
According to the laws and regulations and procedures for the election of senators, the resolution of complaints related to the initial voter list has three levels: at the Capital and Provincial Election Commission, at the National Election Committee, and at the Constitutional Council. The decision of the Constitutional Council is a final decision closing the way for protests.
GENEVA: The International Labor Organization (ILO) said on Wednesday that the global unemployment rate will rise slightly by 2024 as the organization becomes increasingly concerned about stagnant productivity, worsening inequality and the impact. Revenues due to inflation.
According to the AFP news agency, the UN employment agency said the economic recovery from the Kovid-19 pandemic was slowing amid geopolitical tensions and chronic inflation that caused the central bank to suffer. Other countries are taking drastic action.
The ILO says global economic growth in 2023 is slightly higher than expected and the labor market has shown surprising resilience. However, the ILO added that real wages fell in most G20 countries due to lower wages than inflation.
The global unemployment rate in 2022 was 5.3 percent and fell slightly to 5.1 percent in 2023. By 2024, an additional 2 million people are expected to seek employment, raising the global unemployment rate to 5.2 percent.
