One Book, One Community Campaign Supports Community Library Reopening in Battambang
(Battambang): The One Book, One Community Campaign provided support to the Suon Panha Community Library, located in Kampov Village, Phnom Sampov Commune, Banan District, Battambang Province, after the library closed in 2020 due to the 19th Korean Crisis. Community Library Support is a library development program of the One Book, One Community Campaign.
Suon Panha Community Library was officially opened in 2018 to provide opportunities for children and youth in the community to gain knowledge through reading books and programs. Suon Panha Community Library is one of the community libraries across the country that was closed after the Kovid 19 crisis. Currently, those libraries, including the Suon Panha Community Library, have not reopened.
Mr. Sok Lak, Co-Founder of One Book, One Community and Founder of the Intellectual Library, said that the two-day mission to Battambang aims to share books, study materials and share love with children and youth in the community. Live. He added that the main goal is to support community libraries such as the Suon Panha Community Library to be able to reopen the library.
He confirmed, "Today, we brought young people and other relevant partners to see in person in Battambang, located at Suon Panha Library. We give them extra books so that they have more ability or more books to choose from for the public and children. We also came to learn more because during Kovid, some libraries did not work. "So we will talk to them to support the reopening of the library."
"The direct arrival of the One Book, One Community Campaign team is an advantage that they can bring as a library or as a book to the community, especially the children in this rural area," she said. When the community sees this supportive activity, it can show that our youth are connected to promote reading in Cambodia. ”
Noeun Tith Satya, a ninth-grade student at Samdech Ov Samdech Mey Secondary School in Banan District, is enjoying his studies and wants his community to have a library again. Tith Satya continued, “When there was a program at the library, I came to join him. I want my community to have a library again because it is close to my house. ”
Although the library has not reopened, Panha claims that youth groups regularly gather young people and children to read books, as she expects the public to continue to support the library in her community.
Sieng Chanra, a guardian living in Kampov village, also said that most of the children in the area like to play on the phone. She said that when children "come to read books at the library, they cut down on playing on the phone." So she wants the library reopened. ”
By 2023, the One Book One Community Campaign has supported 28 communities across the country, and by 2024, the One Book One Community Campaign aims to strengthen 60 libraries nationwide.
