(Phnom Penh): Although the Democratic Kampuchea regime of 3 years, 8 months and 20 days from April 17, 1975 to January 7, 1979, has been gone for 45 years, but the scars of the disease Capture and great suffering in this regime can not erase the hearts and memories of the Cambodian people who went through this regime.
The Democratic Kampuchea regime, led by Pol Pot, Ieng Sary and Khieu Samphan, has been hailed as one of the most brutal killing fields in Cambodia, killing nearly 3 million Cambodians, students and intellectuals. In addition to the killings, as well as deaths due to lack of food and health problems, many Cambodian families have been displaced, leaving a tragedy to this day.
Ms. Mom described that the evacuation from Phnom Penh in the first year did not seem to be very difficult, as she was able to cope with some of the labor during that regime. But the year after she and her husband were evacuated back to Takeo, her life and that of her family were plunged into unforgettable misery.
In addition to her family being killed, Oum Mom was also jailed for allegedly trying to flee to Vietnam. "I have been subjected to all kinds of hardships. I have been tortured and imprisoned because they want to separate me. I want to flee to Vietnam. All my friends have suffered. I have lost my youth and my family."
This brutal regime deprived her of all her family and relatives, and after her release on January 7, 1979, she had to support her orphaned nephew because the regime even killed her. Ms. Mom said she could not forget this brutal regime and every time she traveled through Takeo, she always remembered this painful history.
On January 7, 2018, tomorrow is the 39th anniversary of the overthrow of the genocidal regime of Pol Pot. Ms. O Mom considers January 7 to be the most auspicious day for her life because this day has revived her life.
In addition to commemorating January 7, Ms. Oum also expressed her appreciation for the current leadership of the Royal Government, which is making the country prosperous. Ms. O Mom, whose life was suffering during the Khmer Rouge regime, wants to see Cambodians in the present era, especially all young people must unite to develop the country to be more prosperous.
