SEOUL: The South Korean government will focus its attention on helping people live and manage risk factors while lowering its 2024 GDP forecast and raising its inflation forecast. . According to Reuters.
The economy will grow 2.2 percent in 2024, down from the projected 2.4 percent, Reuters reported on Thursday, citing the South Korean Ministry of Finance's annual economic policy plan. In July last year, after rising 1.4 percent in 2023, the lowest rate in three years.
The Korean Ministry of Finance expects consumer prices to rise 2.6 percent this year, up from a previous estimate of 2.3 percent. By 2023, consumer prices will rise 3.6 percent.
"The economic recovery will be strong (over the past year) amid improving global trade and demand for semiconductor equipment, but domestic demand and people's livelihoods will be stronger," the ministry said. "People will suffer because of inflation and rising interest rates."
The government will focus on economic recovery for the general population while managing potential risk factors, the finance ministry added.
