BEIJING (Reuters) - China said on Wednesday that the tightening of national security restrictions on global economic growth due to "politicalization of economic and trade issues" would be "immoral and unethical." Sustainability ”after warning from the International Monetary Fund (IMF). According to Reuters.
Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin was quoted as saying by Reuters: "Trade wars, wars of science and technology, secessionist or withdrawal efforts to reduce risks are politicized. "Economy and trade."
"This is immoral and unsustainable and ultimately affects the common interests of the international community," he said.
China and the United States, the world's two largest economies, used to be each other's largest trading partners. Although the two governments have publicly argued that there should be no secession, China is now trading more with Southeast Asia and the United States is increasing trade with its neighbors Canada and Mexico. .
China has introduced measures to curb exports of some key minerals, such as graphite, which China supplies about 67 percent of the world's supply, citing national security concerns.
Meanwhile, the United States has restricted exports of semiconductors and other electronics to China, citing safety concerns.
"China is willing to work with all parties to achieve a comprehensive economic globalization, firmly opposed to anti-globalization policies and against all forms of unilateralism and protectionism," Wang added. "Popular."
