(Siem Reap): The APSARA Authority has cooperated with the local authorities to clear the forest around the site of the Kork Balang temple in Boeung village, Sangkat Chreav, Siem Reap city, and plan to excavate archeological sites and restore the surrounding area for conservation work.
Mr. Som Kakada, Technical Officer of the Department of Temple Preservation and Archeology of the APSARA Authority, said that under the guidance of the leadership of the APSARA Authority, his team from January 2, 2024, came down to the Kork Throne Temple.
After the cooperation of local authorities and nearby residents, reports of abuses were reported by pouring soil on the waterfall next to the hill of the temple. He said that at present, a team of more than 20 members has cleared the forest, planted soup and cleared around the ancient hill site of Kork Balang temple and removed the rubble from the site. This work is scheduled to take 30 days to complete.
He said that on the hill site of the temple, there are still the main door, the throne and some windows, the team has not yet determined the period of construction of the temple. But after the clean-up work, the Apsara Authority plans to excavate the archaeological site and restore the surrounding area for conservation work.
On behalf of the Apsara Authority, Mr. Som Kakkada requested all people living around the ancient hills to take care not to throw garbage, not to abuse on those sites to avoid losing the heritage of Khmer ancestors.
