(Phnom Penh): Oknha Sin Chanthy, President of the Cambodian Logistics Association, said that connecting transportation from agricultural production areas to export ports such as ports or airports is an important factor in boosting exports.
Speaking at Fresh Business, Tycoon Sin Chanthy said that Cambodia's infrastructure is now better, but the potential areas for agricultural production for export do not seem to have high-speed infrastructure connected to ports or airports. Exports yet, while shipping costs currently contribute about 30% of production costs, making Cambodia's agricultural products more expensive than neighboring countries.
The lack of infrastructure connection from agricultural production areas to the export port is one of the reasons why most of Cambodia's unprocessed agricultural products are exported to neighboring countries.
He said that in fact, in the province of Battambang, Pailin, Banteay Meanchey, a province that grows agricultural products for export, such as corn, cassava, longan, Pailin, etc. is far from the port area and there is no high-speed railway for transporting agricultural products to the export port yet.
Mr. Oknha stated that in terms of water transport, Cambodia does not yet have a deep-sea port to receive large ports for transporting goods, while air transport, as well as Cambodia does not yet have large-scale cargo aircraft. Yet, due to the fact that each shipment has to use several stages, while the shipment of goods is small, making the shipment more expensive compared to neighboring countries.
In Cambodia, according to the World Bank report, the association's research shows that Cambodia's logistics costs have risen to 26.6 per cent, while Vietnam is only 20 per cent, Thailand 14 per cent and Singapore 14 per cent. Only 8-9 percent.
Mr. Oknha Sin Chanthy stated that for land transport by train and by car, it is expensive because we only have transportation, which means that if you rent a truck to a certain location. We only had the goods transported, but when we returned there was no cargo, it was empty, so the cost of land transport was still high.
