Chinese and US warships held naval exercises this week amid growing tensions over US ally the Philippines.
The Chinese People's Army (PLA) said on Wednesday that the two-day deployment of its navy and air force, scheduled for completion on Thursday, was carrying out "routine patrols" in The sea.
The report did not specify the location of the patrol and did not provide specific details about its purpose. But it comes at a time when the United States and the Philippines are also holding two days of exercises in disputed waters with China.
Chinese Foreign Ministry spokesman Wang Wenbin said the exercise "undermined the management and control of maritime conditions and related disputes." "We urge the countries concerned to immediately stop their irresponsible activities and return to respecting the efforts of regional countries to restore peace and stability in the South China Sea," he added.
He pledged that Beijing would continue to vigorously defend its territorial sovereignty and maritime rights and interests.
Beijing claims sovereignty over almost the entire South China Sea and has ignored the International Court of Justice ruling that its claims have no legal basis.
