Therefore, the Ministry of Health calls on all citizens once again to take care of themselves with a high sense of responsibility to prevent the spread of chickenpox by understanding how to infect and to take measures. Prevent the following:
1. Transmission: * The chickenpox virus is transmitted from person to person through all forms of sexual contact with a person with chickenpox and direct contact with wounds, body fluids, saliva, airborne droplets, and contaminated equipment. Viruses include clothing, bedding, towels or utensils, spoons, plates, glasses of water, etc.
2. Measures to prevent the spread of chickenpox: * Practice safe sex with a condom every time you have sex and avoid having multiple sexual partners.
Avoid direct contact, such as hugging, kissing, massage, talking to people with symptoms of chickenpox.
Avoid direct or indirect contact with clothing, bedding, pillows or towels, or bowls, spoons or cups, or surfaces of areas where you have chickenpox.
* Individuals who suspect or confirm that they have chickenpox must be kept in isolation until the rash is gone.
* Individuals who provide care for patients with chickenpox must use appropriate personal protective measures.
* Individuals with symptoms such as rash with blisters on the face, palms, feet, body, eyes, mouth or genitals, fever, swollen lymph nodes, headache, muscle aches and low back pain, fatigue, even direct contact with patients with chickenpox or no history of exposure. With chickenpox patients, consult a health center or hospital for a check-up or test for chickenpox or call 115 for advice and consultation.
3. For health workers: * Raise awareness and education of caregivers, as well as implement prevention and control of infections in hospitals or health centers in accordance with the technical specifications, especially be careful of exposure and droplets. Adherence to hand hygiene, personal protective equipment, proper handling of contaminated medical equipment, proper cleaning of infected waste, as well as cleaning and disinfecting the environment.
* All hospitals and health centers must be vigilant and closely monitor patients with skin rashes or abnormal blisters, fever and symptoms as described above, please report as soon as possible to prevent Transmission in families and communities.
Implement infection prevention measures during consultation and immediately isolate suspected cases or cases of chickenpox in separate, well-ventilated rooms and separate bathrooms.
It should be reminded that the Ministry of Health found the first case of chickenpox in a 28-year-old man living in Pesey village, Sangkat Teuk Thla, Khan Sen Sok, Phnom Penh on December 13, 2023.
