General Sar Thet urges police officers to strengthen technology-related capacity as more people suffer from this crime
(Phnom Penh): General Sar Thet, General Commissioner of the National Police, instructed all heads of departments, units, departments and units of the Capital-Provincial Police, as well as all officers, to strengthen their capacity and keep up with the digital technology society in the past and so far. Many people are still affected by technology scams, especially in rural areas.
In the meeting to disseminate the indicators of key measures, digital economic and social policy framework 2021-2035 and financial technology development 2023-2028 of the General Commission of the National Police on the afternoon of January 2, 2024, General Sar Thet observed that at the time of the disaster, people often went to the local level to file a complaint, but the ability of local officials to rescue seems to be limited.
Seeing this challenge, the General Commission of the National Police invited the Chief of the Unit under the General Commission of the National Police, especially the Capital-Provincial Police Commissioner to listen to the dissemination and listen to the presentation on the management and research on technological crimes to Help the victims. This is according to General Sar Thet.
"In order for us to understand the management, prevention and investigation of this crime, we need to have an understanding of technology to facilitate communication with specialized departments to help each other," said General Sar Thet. "We have to get our officers not yet trained on these issues, so there are a lot of problems."
According to the General Commissioner of the National Police, the areas with the highest number of people affected by technology crimes are in Mondulkiri, Ratanakkiri, Siem Reap and Phnom Penh. Therefore, all police officers must strengthen their capacity to increase their confidence. People on the leadership of the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia and units under the Royal Government of Cambodia.
General Sar Thet added that once the police officers are trained and capable enough to prevent and crack down, they can explain to the people the tricks of the criminals' scams. He said that when the authorities control this crime, it will be possible to successfully crack down on other crimes involved.
