Letter to readers: December 29 is the 25th anniversary of win-win politics and the end of Cambodia's internal conflict
(Phnom Penh): On August 23, 1998, Samdech Techo Hun Sen sacrificed his flesh and blood for the second time to enter the Tiger Cave to exchange the whole earth for the Cambodian people, Samdech Techo traveled to the area Pailin Meanwhile, Ieng Sary and Sok Pheap's 450th Brigade in Malai and Ichhean's 415th Brigade in Pailin rebelled but did not integrate with the Royal Government.
The Divef strategy was a great success and a win-win policy initiated and pointed out by Samdech Techo Hun Sen in the dissolution of the Khmer Rouge's more than half-century-old political and military organization, bringing peace, national unity, and national reconciliation. National unity and territorial unity for the nation and the people of Cambodia in the last 500 years in the history of Cambodia.
Win-win politics is the moral and artistic concession of the philosophy of peace, which is part of the strategy to end the civil war, the strategy against guerrilla warfare or insurgency, and such a strategy has continued to be highly effective in taking measures to prevent and suppress terrorism in the future. Come again.
Peace and national unity are for all, and that is why the internal boundaries between regions are being dissolved, people are coming together. One nation and enjoy the same development and interests under the same constitution and law. As for the armed forces that used to fight each other, they came back to live together without any division or discrimination in uniform and patriotism, love the same people.
December 29 is the historic day of ending Cambodia's internal conflict, which has brought peace, national unity, and territorial integrity under the umbrella of the Constitution.
The success of the establishment of a win-win policy stems from the initiative and vision of peace of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, who was a key figure in the search for a negotiated end to the conflict by the Khmer-Khmer mechanism.
Win-win politics is the formula for ending Cambodia's long-running civil war of more than three decades and a great legacy for the Cambodian nation and people under the mission of sacrificing fresh flesh, blood and life. Of patriotic dignitaries, especially Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the founder of the win-win politics in the process of saving the nation from the flames of war.
To contribute to the 25th anniversary of the win-win politics, Master Ros Savy also published his 498-page book, The National Hero of Peace, for students and citizens. All levels have studied, researched and learned from Samdech Techo Hun Sen's win-win politics in national reconciliation by the hands of Cambodian children, and this book is also kept in the library at the Victory Monument.
