Ministry of Information to re-organize children's music and song festival for the purpose of moral training for Cambodian children
(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Information, through the National Television of Cambodia, will re-organize the Children's Music and Song Festival for the purpose of educating and cultivating ideas and morals for Cambodian children, especially to promote children's rights, eliminate child exploitation, eradicate drug abuse and raise awareness. About traffic safety.
It should be reminded that the children's music and song contest has been held since the mid-1980s by the National Television of Cambodia. Later, the Children's Music and Song Festival was organized by the Ministry of Information and within the framework of cooperation with relevant ministries, NGOs and the private sector.
In order to organize this festival smoothly, on December 26, 2023, Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Minister of Information, chaired a meeting of the Organizing Committee of the Children's Music and Song Festival as part of the celebration of International Children's Day 2024.
On that occasion, the Minister urged the organizing committee to disseminate widely so that the public and national and international organizations, companies, enterprises and program sponsors receive information and understand the value of organizing the festival to mobilize participation. Collaborate to support both spirit, budget and material.
According to a spokesman for the Ministry of Information, the purpose of the festival is to promote the participation of Cambodian children in showing their true potential. Through this festival, it is also an opportunity to encourage Cambodian children to show their courage, talent and achievements to set a good example for other children, who are all bamboo shoots for the society.
The organizing committee of the Children's Music and Song Festival Award of the Ministry of Information will hold further meetings to discuss and develop a detailed plan to make the festival a success in support of the priority policies of the Royal Government. 100 poor families
