Mr. Nhem Sam Oeun handed over the Royal Government's decision to the chairpersons of the 9 districts and Ratanakkiri provincial relief teams. Ambok Khmer, Battambang province, is preparing to appear on the international market after implementing standard technology, production and taste support.
(Battambang): Ambok, a type of food of the Cambodian people since ancient times, but more importantly, the ambok of Battambang people are ready to export to the international market after adhering to the standards in production and have Taste support.
Nhem Chanty, director of Angkor Meas in Romchek 2 village, Rattanak commune, Battambang city, Battambang province, told reporters that her place produces five kinds of ready-made ambok, such as ambok seaweed. Sea ambok, mixed fruits, special ambok, banana ambok and natural ambok. As for dried fish, there are natural fish, Tonle Sap fish, Battambang fish, Hil fish, sesame fish.
She stated that the dried fish can be stored for a long time and does not spoil the quality because it is in accordance with the standards accepted by the Ministry of Commerce and relevant ministries.
She stated, "From 2017 to the present, there is a lot of support and encouragement, I can not imagine, because a kind of food, which is a traditional Khmer food, has been around for a long time and is celebrated. "Every year."
However, Ms. Nhem Chanty claimed that in the current period, the number of people who eat ambok seems to decrease, especially in the group of adults, but the younger generation still has a high rate of eating. However, she said that the fact that adults do not like to eat or have little support for ambok may be due to the idea of unhygienic production. That is why she made many kinds of ambok to regain support by making ambok from fragrant rice.
In connection with the production of this ambok, she claimed that it was different from the past, which in the past was not modern, but now she is modern using a machine, and the people who produce this are all hygienic.
Ms. Nhem Chanty said that the standard production of ambok takes 24 hours to 48 hours (making ambok), then follow the production process until the selection of one kilogram of ambok takes 1 hour to put in the box for Take to market. The Ministry of Commerce and the Ministry of Handicrafts, Science, Technology and Innovation are preparing to provide intellectual property for sale to international markets.
Ms. Nhem Chanty confirmed that the ambok will be ready to sell to markets such as Australia, China, Korea and Japan. But now it is selling in the local market. All the rice used to make ambok is collected by farmers. As for the price, one bite weighs almost $ 3.
According to the owner of the production, the Ministry of Agriculture is now preparing to produce one type of rice for making ambok, 70 Champey rice, which is a high quality rice.
"Once we have the machine, we can produce 1 ton per day and we are ready to pack for export," she said. Strong wind destroys more than 1,000 houses in Prey Nup district, Mr. Kouch Chamroeun brings relief supplies to victims
